<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tarumanagara</h1><p class="st-meta">358 CE – 669 CE (approx.) · capital Sundapura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first known kingdom of Java, whose King Purnawarman left Sanskrit inscriptions around Bogor and Jakarta in the 5th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The kingdom is known chiefly from seven stone inscriptions in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallava-script">Pallava script</a> and dated on palaeographic grounds to the 5th century. The register's founding date of 358 comes from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangsakerta-manuscripts">Wangsakerta manuscripts</a>, whose reliability historians doubt. Chinese records of embassies from To-lo-mo between 528 and 669 probably refer to it.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Purnawarman compared himself to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and left carved footprints on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciaruteun-inscription">Ciaruteun</a> stone. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tugu-inscription">Tugu inscription</a> records a canal more than 11 kilometres long dug in 21 days, completed with a gift of 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, evidence of organised labour and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> ritual. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a> farming and coastal trade supported the state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Sundanese tradition holds that the kingdom split around 669 into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/galuh-kingdom">Galuh Kingdom</a>, separated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/citarum-river">Citarum River</a>. Its name lives on in the Tarum River, and its inscriptions are among the oldest written records of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tarumanagara.jpg" alt="Tarumanagara" width="320" height="163"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarumanagara.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>358 CE – 669 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sundapura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Purnawarman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit (inscriptions), Old Sundanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Taruma, Tarumanegara</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=514&amp;focus=tarumanagara" aria-label="Tarumanagara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7641 2414 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tarumanagara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7961 2670L7999 2702"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7999 2702h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7961 2670h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7961 2670h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=514&amp;focus=tarumanagara">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
